    related: ['size-solar-pumping-worked-example', 'store-water-or-batteries-solar-pumping', 'choose-solar-pump-borehole-guide'],
    sections: [
      { h: `Why start from water needs before choosing a pump`, body: `<p>A solar pumping system is sized from a single figure: <strong>the volume of water to deliver each day, in m³ per day</strong>. The pump, the solar array, the cable cross-section and the basin size all follow from that number and the total dynamic head. If the volume is underestimated, the basin will not fill in the hot season; if it is overestimated, you pay for a pump and panels you do not need.</p><p>The values in this article are <strong>general orders of magnitude</strong>, to be adjusted for crop, soil, climate and irrigation method. A visit with a flow test on your borehole remains the only way to validate a sizing.</p>` },
      { h: `Market gardening: 30 to 50 m³ per hectare per day with drip irrigation`, body: `<p>For market gardening irrigated by drip, a common order of magnitude is <strong>30 to 50 m³ per hectare per day</strong>. As a reminder, 1 mm of water over 1 hectare is 10 m³, so this range equals 3 to 5 mm per day. Real needs vary widely:</p><ul><li><strong>Start of cycle, young plants</strong>: low end of the range.</li><li><strong>Fully grown crop in peak heat</strong>: high end of the range, sometimes beyond.</li><li><strong>Sprinkler or gravity irrigation</strong>: higher than drip because of losses.</li></ul><p>For two hectares under drip, that means <strong>60 to 100 m³ per day</strong>: the peak period is what sizes the installation.</p>` },
      { h: `Livestock: a few litres per head per day`, body: `<p>Watering livestock uses much less water than irrigation, but it is steady and cannot be interrupted. Indicative orders of magnitude in a hot climate:</p><ul><li><strong>Cattle</strong>: about 40 to 60 litres per head per day, more for a lactating cow or in strong heat.</li><li><strong>Small ruminants (sheep, goats)</strong>: about 5 to 10 litres per head per day.</li></ul><p>One hundred cattle therefore need <strong>4 to 6 m³ per day</strong>, and two hundred small ruminants <strong>1 to 2 m³ per day</strong>. Because animals drink mostly at certain hours, a buffer basin or trough smooths demand.</p>` },
      { h: `Add up, then add a 20% margin`, body: `<p>The method takes four steps:</p><ol><li>List each use (crops, livestock, household).</li><li>Multiply the quantity (hectares, heads) by the unit need.</li><li>Add all uses together.</li><li>Add about <strong>20% margin</strong> for leaks, basin evaporation and future growth.</li></ol><p><strong>Mixed farm example:</strong> 1 hectare of drip-irrigated market gardening at 40 m³ per day, 40 cattle at 50 litres (2 m³) and 60 small ruminants at 7.5 litres (0.45 m³). Total: 42.45 m³ per day. With a 20% margin: 42.45 × 1.2 ≈ <strong>51 m³ per day</strong>.</p>` },
      { h: `From 51 m³ per day to a pumping system: the worked example`, body: `<p>Assume a total dynamic head of 45 m (pumping depth, losses and discharge height combined). With the assumptions of our <a href='pompage-solaire.html#simulateur'>pre-sizing tool</a> (5.5 equivalent sun hours per day, overall efficiency of 0.4, array losses of 0.8):</p><ul><li><strong>Hydraulic energy</strong>: 2.725 × 51 × 45 ≈ 6,254 Wh per day.</li><li><strong>Solar array</strong>: 6,254 ÷ (0.4 × 0.8 × 5.5) ≈ 3,553 Wp, i.e. <strong>3.6 kWp</strong>, about 6 panels of 600 Wp.</li><li><strong>Peak flow</strong> (pumping over 6 hours): 51 ÷ 6 = <strong>8.5 m³/h</strong>.</li><li><strong>Pump power</strong>: 2.725 × 8.5 × 45 ÷ 0.5 ≈ <strong>2.1 kW</strong>.</li></ul><p>These figures fall within the limits of the <a href='catalogue.html#pompe-dc-3000'>3,000 W DC solar pump</a> in the catalogue (28 m³/h maximum, 55 m maximum head). This is a first estimate: the visit will confirm the real borehole yield.</p>` },
      { h: `Pitfalls to avoid`, body: `<ul><li><strong>Confusing borehole depth with total dynamic head</strong>: you must add pipe friction losses and the discharge height up to the basin.</li><li><strong>Ignoring the borehole yield</strong>: the pump must not pump more than the borehole can give. The flow test is there for that.</li><li><strong>Sizing for the cool season</strong>: it is the hot season, with a grown crop, that sets the peak need.</li><li><strong>Neglecting storage</strong>: a basin or water tower absorbs the peak and lets you pump when the sun is out.</li></ul>` },
      { h: `Have it validated by a flow test`, body: `<p>Before you order, a SOLARIG technician measures the dynamic level and the flow of your borehole on site, then recalculates the pump and the solar array with your real figures. You can start with the <a href='pompage-solaire.html#simulateur'>pre-sizing tool</a>, then <a href='contact.html#devis'>request a quote</a> stating your daily water volume, the borehole depth and your location. To learn more about our services, see the <a href='pompage-solaire.html'>solar pumping</a> page.</p>` },
    ],
    faq: [
      { q: `How much water does 1 hectare of market gardening need?`, a: `With drip irrigation, a common order of magnitude is 30 to 50 m³ per hectare per day, i.e. 3 to 5 mm per day. Needs vary with crop, growth stage, season and soil: they are higher in peak heat and with sprinklers.` },
      { q: `How much water does a cow drink per day?`, a: `About 40 to 60 litres per day in a hot climate, more for a lactating cow or in strong heat. Small ruminants (sheep, goats) drink about 5 to 10 litres per head per day.` },
      { q: `Which solar pump for 50 m³ per day?`, a: `It depends on the total dynamic head. For 51 m³ per day at 45 m, our estimate gives about 3.6 kWp of panels, a peak flow of 8.5 m³/h and a pump power of 2.1 kW, compatible with the 3,000 W DC pump in the catalogue. A visit with a flow test confirms the choice.` },
      { q: `Do you need a basin for agricultural solar pumping?`, a: `It is strongly recommended. A solar pump runs when the sun shines: a basin or water tower stores water for distribution on demand, more simply and at lower cost than batteries.` },
    ],
